  "query_preview": "(This evening through Saturday)...\nIssued at 239 PM EDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nRest of This Afternoon and Tonight...\n\nA weakening cold front will continue to sink into the area into \ntonight. Temperatures in the middle 80s to around 90 combined with \ndewpoints in the lower to middle 70s have aided in creating MLCAPE \nvalues between 2000-3000 J/kg. However, shear is weak. Although the \nfront doesn't have any upper support, it might be able to tap into \nthe instability and generate some isolated convection. \n\nWill continue with mainly slight chance PoPs (isolated wording) into \nearly this evening as the front stalls out in the area. With the \nlack of shear, severe storms are not expected. \n\nOvernight, light winds and mostly clear skies will allow some fog to \ndevelop across the area. Widespread dense fog looks unlikely, but \nfavored areas may see brief dense fog. \n\nSlightly cooler air behind the front will allow the far northeast \nforecast area to get low temperatures in the lower 60s, but most \nareas will be in the mid 60s to around 70.\n\nSunday and Sunday night...\n\nFog will mix out early Sunday morning. Otherwise, Sunday morning \nwill be quiet. Sunday afternoon, remnant thunderstorms or new \nthunderstorms created from outflow from remnant thunderstorms to the \nwest of the area may move in/develop. Best forcing will be north of \nthe area, so kept PoPs no higher than the chance category and near/ \nnorth of I-70. Confidence is low in coverage not only because the \nbest forcing will be north of the area, but also whether outflow \nfrom the storms may spark additional storms to the south.\n\nSunday night, more convection will develop northwest of central \nIndiana on the nose of an 850mb jet and with some upper energy. \nThese storms will move southeast and may get into the northern \nforecast area. Will have some low PoPs there. Will have to watch and \nsee if the storms can build farther south than expected. \n\nShear will be better on Sunday, so some isolated severe storms are \npossible, mainly north.\n\nSunday will be hot and humid, with heat indices approaching 100 \ndegrees across the south.\n\nMonday onward...\n\nAn upper level high/ridge will remain to the west and south of \ncentral Indiana through much of the period. Energy riding around the \nhigh will bring forcing to the area at times. Meanwhile, at the \nsurface, a front will meander in the vicinity. \n\nFor much of the week, ample moisture will be in place. Surface \ndewpoints will be in the 70s. Forcing from upper energy and the \nsurface front will produce scattered to numerous showers and \nthunderstorms at times into next weekend.\n\nStrong instability should be able to build at times into mid-week \nnext week, and shear will increase at times. These will keep the \nthreat for severe weather around at times into mid-week next week. \nThe primary severe threat looks to be damaging winds. The plentiful \nmoisture content of the atmosphere will also keep heavy rain and \nlocalized flooding a threat.\n\nHowever, uncertainty on the timing and strength of the upper energy \nmoving around the upper high leads to uncertainty on timing and \ncoverage of severe storms. For now, the threat looks to gradually \nshift south across the area into mid-week next week as the upper \nridge gets pushed farther south. \n\nLate week next week into next weekend, the threat for convection \nwill continue, but with some milder air sneaking into the area, \ninstability will be lower, leading to a lower severe threat.\n\nLooking at temperatures, the proximity of the upper high will lead \nto hot conditions early next week, with highs around 90. Lows will \nonly be in the 70s. With the dewpoints in the 70s, heat indices will \nbe around 100 degrees. Heat Risk may reach the Moderate category for \nparts of the area on Monday and Tuesday. Of course, the \naforementioned thunderstorms could easily impact how warm it gets, \nadding to the uncertainty. Will have to watch for the potential for \na Heat Advisory Monday and Tuesday, mainly south where the warmer \ntemperatures and lower chances for rain will be.\n\nTemperatures will moderate into the end of next week, with highs in \nthe upper 70s to lower 80s expected.",
